{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Re-censused on `origin/main` at `50d5a36539d` before filing children. The epic's description was verified against `083620b0`; main has moved and 2 of its numbers have drifted.\n\n**Drift found:**\n\n| Epic says | Actual at `50d5a36539d` |\n|---|---|\n| 54 importers of the `_engine_support/resolver.py` shim | 51 |\n| `reducer/_processors.py:18-31` re-exports | the re-exports now span `:21-30`, and are 7 names, not the implied block |\n\n**A grep-shaped census of the `_processors` re-exports is wrong, and was wrong here.** A line-oriented `git grep -F 'from rebar.reducer._processors import <name>'` reports **0** importers for `process_key_event` and misses `tests/unit/test_attestations_reducer.py` for two other names. The reason is that `src/rebar/reducer/_replay.py:19` and that test import inside multi-line parenthesized statements, so the imported name never shares a line with the module path. The grep's answer — \"4 of 7 names are dead, the rest are test-only\" — is false in both halves: `_replay.py` is **production** code importing 3 of them.\n\nThe correct instrument is an AST parse of every `.py` under `src tests scripts`, resolving relative imports and separately scanning `ast.Attribute` for module-attribute access. That census is recorded in full on story `censurable-exotic-snake` and gives:\n\n- 4 names with zero importers: `_fold_claimed_session`, `_fold_close_metadata`, `_fold_plan_review_phase`, `_rederive_keyring_keys`.\n- `process_key_event` 1 site, `process_signature` 5, `process_status` 6 — each including `_replay.py:19`.\n- No attribute-style access anywhere.\n\nThis is the same class of blind spot that produced a duplicate slice in epic 87d8, in a different disguise: there, censusing one worktree hid a sibling branch's work; here, a line-oriented pattern hides a multi-line import. Both come from choosing an instrument that cannot see the shape the code actually takes.\n\n**S1 is being filed as two independent stories rather than one**, under the standing authorization to size tickets properly. They share no source file and neither depends on the other:\n\n- `2207-e956-c937-4e8c` (`insanitary-divine-moa`) — resolver shim: 51 importers, a pure module-path rewrite with no symbol renaming, since all 9 re-exported names are identical on both sides and nothing reaches the module as a string.\n- `19ba-bd62-1adb-41df` (`censurable-exotic-snake`) — `_processors` re-exports: 9 files, with 3 import statements needing splitting because they also pull `process_snapshot`, which genuinely lives in `_processors.py`.\n\nBoth carry the epic's criterion 1. The remaining children S2-S5 are not yet filed; S2 additionally depends on the overlap epic's `overlap_*` removal story, which still needs locating."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793377829976438023,"uuid":"e0948480-4930-464d-9013-ba3b0f104edf"}